<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castor and Pollux</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dioscuri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castor and Pollux, the Dioscuri, were twin heroes and gods of Greek and Roman religion, sons of Leda and, in most accounts, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. They were patrons of horsemen and protectors of sailors. Roman legend held that they fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the battle of Lake Regillus around 496 BCE and then appeared in the Forum, where a temple was dedicated to them; their cult was strong at nearby Tusculum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux.jpg" alt="Castor and Pollux" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castor_Pollux_anagoria.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Castor and Pollux, or Polydeuces, are twin half-brothers whose mother was Leda. The sources disagree about their parentage. In the Homeric Odyssey they are sons of Tyndareus alone, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and in the Hesiodic Catalogue of Women sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. Pindar's Nemean Odes gives the conventional account, in which only Pollux was fathered by Zeus, which explains why Pollux was granted immortality and Castor was mortal. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, Helen says that her brothers are dead and buried at home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Lacedaemon</a>, which suggests that in some early traditions both were mortal. Scholars connect them with the Indo-European horse twins, such as the Vedic Ashvins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Both brothers were excellent horsemen and hunters. They took part in the hunt of the Calydonian Boar and joined the crew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argo</a>, on which Pollux defeated King Amycus of the Bebryces in a boxing contest. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> abducted their sister Helen, they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> to rescue her and took Theseus's mother Aethra to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> as her slave.</p><p>They then carried off the Leucippides, Phoebe and Hilaeira, who were betrothed to their cousins Idas and Lynceus. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raid ended in a quarrel, and Idas fatally wounded Castor with a spear. Pollux killed Lynceus, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> struck Idas dead with a thunderbolt. Zeus then offered Pollux a choice between all of Olympus and sharing his immortality with his dying brother. Pollux chose to share it, so the twins alternate between Olympus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hades">Hades</a>; they became the two brightest stars of the constellation Gemini and were said to appear to sailors as St. Elmo's fire. They were regarded as patrons of travellers, sailors, horsemen and athletes, intervening at moments of crisis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The twins were important at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. A symbol of them was two upright posts joined by crossbars, called dokana, and it was carried before the army in the field. Their grave-shrine was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/therapne">Therapne</a>, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurotas">Eurotas</a> from Sparta. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> venerated them as Kastur and Pultuce.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> revered them from the 5th century BCE, probably through the Greek colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a>. According to legend the twins fought at the head of the Roman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-regillus">Battle of Lake Regillus</a> and brought the news of victory to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the temple of Castor and Pollux was built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a> to fulfil a vow made after that battle. On 15 July, their feast day, 1,800 equestrians paraded through Rome. After the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Pope Gelasius I in the 5th century still attested a cult of the Castores that people did not want to abandon, and the church adopted Saints Peter and Paul in place of the twins as patrons of travellers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castor and Pollux” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its end stands Al-Khazneh, the Treasury, 24 metres wide and 37 metres tall, its façade carved with a broken pediment around a central tholos, the twins <b>Castor and Pollux</b>, and a female figure believed to be Isis-Tyche.</blockquote><a href="/work/petra">Petra · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>A son of the dictator Aulus Postumius dedicated the temple his father had vowed before the victory at Lake Regillus, on the spot where, by legend, the <b>Dioscuri</b> had appeared in the Forum.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-15">15 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Afterwards Tusculum became one of Rome's most loyal allies in the wars against the Aequi and Volsci, and it hosted shrines of <b>Castor and Pollux</b>, gods also linked to Regillus.</blockquote><a href="/state/tusculum">Tusculum</a></li></ul></section>
